<https://dataout.org/rdf/data/project-6-21#record_8bc545c3> <https://dataout.org/ontology#hasSnippet> "The streaming platform “Start.ru” was fined for distributing “Perfect Strangers” (2016, Paolo Genovese). The court held that the film contained depictions and verbal references to same-sex relations between men, including scenes in which one character’s male partner sends intimate messages and the group discusses his sexual orientation. As stated in the ruling, the platform had allowed minors to access content containing “scenes demonstrating (describing) homosexual relations between men, as well as references to homosexual relations,” including dialogue such as: “If my best friend is gay, I want to know it … then I will decide myself how to relate to it.” The scenes identified by the court: 00:58:46–00:59:09 — The court highlighted a phone conversation in which a male caller confronts another male character about emotional intimacy, interpreting it as an explicit reference to same-sex desire: “Why did you leave? … You still cannot decide who you like: men or women.”; 01:01:36–01:03:50 — The ruling focused on a group discussion where friends react to the presumed homosexuality of one of the men, including statements about disclosure, friendship, and social acceptance: “If my best friend is gay, I want to know it … then I will decide myself how to relate to it.”; 01:17:40–01:20:40 — The court separately analysed the later confession scene in which one character openly identifies himself as gay and reflects on fear of disclosure: “The truth is, the faggot is me … How could I even tell you that I am gay?”"@en .
