<https://dataout.org/rdf/data/project-6-21#record_39b03fd4> <https://dataout.org/ontology#hasSnippet> "“MangaLib”, a platform for online comics and manga, was fined for publications on its website. The publication in this case is “Boo! It’s Sex” (Danielle Corsetto, Monica Gallagher, 2018). From the court decision: “This 97-chapter series follows four freshmen – Denise, Christina, Lindsay, and Kim – as they move into a family-owned country house to study for college exams. On their first day, they meet Tara, the ghost of a deceased woman who now inhabits a bottle of tequila. While the girls adjust to their new surroundings, Tara acts as their mentor as they explore their bodies and sexuality. The series contains non-episodic depictions of non-traditional sexual relationships between the characters, represented by several illustrations of two female individuals in a romantic context: kissing, hugging, and sensual touching.” The court stated that “the information displayed can be classified as revealing signs of a socio-cultural threat to the national security of the Russian Federation in terms of the protection of traditional values, since it reveals signs of ideological and psychological influence on the recipient in the form of the imposition of a destructive ideology (propaganda of non-traditional sexual relations).” This is one of at least 12 cases in a serial persecution of “MangaLib”. The manager of “MangaLib” was also prosecuted at least 12 times for different publications."@en .
