<https://dataout.org/rdf/data/project-6-21#record_7bd56650> <https://dataout.org/ontology#hasSnippet> "The publishing house “Eksmo” was fined for publishing and circulating the book “Pigeon” (Koro, 2021). An individual found this book in a public library and handed it over to the police. The court cited a linguistic report stating that the text “contains discussion of non-traditional sexual relations (not between a man and a woman)” and highlighted the age marking “12+” as proof that the text was addressed to readers over twelve. The report noted “references to young men in the description of pigeons,” “a man in the description of one pigeon,” and “men represented through the image of a pigeon and a crow.” A further expert interview characterised the book as mass-distribution printed material aimed, among others, at minors, and claimed that it used “a manipulative strategy of influence,” with “indirect, hidden psychological impact.” The same opinion concluded that the book promoted “a positive emotional-semantic attitude toward non-traditional relations, presented as normal, ordinary, generally accepted, that is, natural,” while allegedly producing a negative emotional attitude toward traditional family and sexual relations."@en .
