<https://dataout.org/rdf/data/project-6-21#record_dcccd598> <https://dataout.org/ontology#hasSnippet> "“MangaLib”, a platform for online comics and manga, was fined for publications on its website. The publication in this case is “Share” (Mitsuha Yuu, 2018). According to the court, this manga “tells the story of a girl named Haru, who has no particular ambitions in life and works part-time at a cafe to save money. One day, Rio, a handsome guy of homosexual orientation, drops by her cafe job. He attracts Haru's attention, and she pursues him all the way to his home. She stays there and cares for him while in a complicated relationship with his boyfriend, Iwakura. The material contains non-episodic depictions of non-traditional sexual relationships between characters, represented by several illustrations of the male protagonists (Rio and Iwakura) in a romantic and sexual context: hugs, kisses, sex, etc.” The court stated that “the information displayed can be classified as revealing signs of a socio-cultural threat to the national security of the Russian Federation in terms of the protection of traditional values, since it reveals signs of ideological and psychological influence on the recipient in the form of the imposition of a destructive ideology (propaganda of non-traditional sexual relations).” This is one of at least 12 cases in a serial persecution of “MangaLib”. The manager of “MangaLib” was also prosecuted at least 12 times for different publications."@en .
